+# Component architecture
+
+## Decision
+
+Refactor the review UI into concrete components. Each component owns its
+state, handles its user and external events, and renders itself.
+
+Keep one synchronous runtime. Do not add a component framework or an async
+runtime. Use the component pattern documented by Ratatui. Send events from
+runtime producers through one Crossbeam channel to a typed component event
+bus.
+
+Consolidating the current synchronous event sources into one Crossbeam
+channel is part of this design. It does not introduce async tasks or change
+which runtime owns external work.
+
+Use Bevy-style typed handler registration. Each component declares its own
+subscriptions beside its handler methods. Do not put the subscription list in
+a central router and do not generate it with a macro.
+
+Separate reusable component infrastructure from product components. Put the
+component trait, typed event bus, opaque component targets, handler results,
+and shared rendering interfaces in a small `component-core` crate.
+This crate must not depend on a concrete review component.
+
+Put each independent product component in its own crate. Keep `review-ui` as
+the application composition crate. Bias toward more small crates with a
+one-way dependency graph so Cargo can compile unrelated components in
+parallel and reuse their build results independently.
+
+## Problem
+
+The current code has render-only views, but it does not have complete UI
+components. State and behavior for one screen area are spread across several
+large files:
+
+- `ReviewApp` owns almost all UI state.
+- `input.rs` handles input for every screen area.
+- `navigation.rs` handles diff, source, and history behavior.
+- files such as `files.rs` and `diff.rs` only render borrowed `ReviewApp`
+  state.
+- `reviewer::runtime` polls and drains several event sources before every
+  redraw.
+
+This shape makes a local change affect unrelated code. It also makes it hard
+to identify which type owns a cursor, a pending request, or an external
+result.
+
+## Goals
+
+- Keep state, event handling, and rendering for one component together.
+- Route user input to the active component.
+- Route an external event only to the component that owns it.
+- Keep all state changes on one UI thread.
+- Keep blocking repository and integration work outside UI components.
+- Preserve the existing typed action boundary for external work.
+- Preserve current behavior, shortcuts, cursor positions, and scroll
+  positions during the refactor.
+- Keep components small enough that their state and behavior can be understood
+  in one module.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Do not move to Tokio or another async runtime.
+- Do not adopt `tuirealm`, `ratatui-kit`, or another UI framework.
+- Do not create one thread or task for each component.
+- Do not let a component read from a shared terminal receiver directly.
+- Do not perform repository, LSP, Herdr, storage, or clipboard work while
+  rendering.
+- Do not create crate dependency cycles or copy shared types to avoid a
+  dependency.
+- Do not put product-specific behavior in the reusable component crate.
+- Do not use string event names or untyped event payloads.
+- Do not capture a component reference in a subscription callback.
+- Do not expose the bus's internal component IDs. Use opaque
+  `ComponentTarget` values for exact component access.
+
+## Target control flow
+
+```text
+ terminal reader ---------+
+ repository watcher ------+
+ repository worker -------+
+ LSP worker --------------+--> Crossbeam channel --> ReviewApplication
+ Herdr event reader -------+                              |
+ guide mailbox watcher ----+                    input eligibility
+ timer --------------------+                              |
+                                                  ComponentEventBus
+                                              declared subscriptions
+                                                          |
+                                           mounted component handlers
+                                                  |
+                                             component actions
+                                                  |
+                                        runtime executes external work
+```
+
+The runtime owns terminal setup, event producers, the central event receiver,
+external action execution, and terminal draws. `ReviewApplication` owns the
+component event bus, layout, focus, and component coordination. Components own
+feature behavior and declare which event types they handle.
+
+## Crate boundaries
+
+Use this target directory structure:
+
+```text
+crates/
+├── component-core/
+├── ui-actions/
+├── ui-events/
+├── components/
+│   ├── files/
+│   ├── diff/
+│   ├── guide/
+│   ├── revision/
+│   ├── status/
+│   └── overlay/
+└── review-ui/
+```
+
+Add `crates/components/*` to the workspace members. Directory names organize
+related crates but do not provide a Cargo namespace. Give the nested packages
+the unique names `files-component`, `diff-component`, `guide-component`,
+`revision-component`, `status-component`, and `overlay-component`.
+
+Use this target dependency shape:
+
+```text
+reviewer
+   |
+   v
+review-ui --------------------------------------+
+   |              |              |              |
+   v              v              v              v
+files-component                    diff-component
+       |                                  |
+       +----------------+-----------------+
+                        |
+guide-component         |         revision-component
+       |                |                 |
+       +----------------+-----------------+
+                        |
+          +-------------+-------------+
+          |             |             |
+          v             v             v
+  component-core    ui-events    ui-actions
+```
+
+The `status-component` and `overlay-component` crates use the same dependency
+direction. The component crates can depend on relevant existing domain crates,
+such as repository, guide, LSP, store, and shared review types. They must reuse
+those types instead of defining UI copies.
+
+`component-core` is library-style code. It owns only general component
+mechanics:
+
+- `Component` and `ApplicationEvent` traits;
+- `ComponentEventBus`, opaque `ComponentTarget` values, and `EventPublisher`;
+- typed subscription builders and erased internal dispatch;
+- target-based component mounting, access, removal, and subscription cleanup;
+- generic input matchers, `DispatchResult<A>`, and result conversion; and
+- small rendering interfaces that do not contain review concepts.
+
+`ui-events` contains the concrete cross-component event vocabulary.
+`ui-actions` contains the external-work requests that components return. The
+generic parameter `A` is `ui_actions::Action` in this application. This keeps
+`component-core` independent from review-specific actions. Create these leaf
+crates only when the first extracted component needs them. Before that point,
+use existing shared types where they already provide the required boundary.
+
+Each product component crate owns its state, event handlers, rendering, and
+adjacent tests. It must not depend on `review-ui` or another component crate.
+Cross-component communication uses event and action types from the leaf
+crates. `review-ui` depends on all component crates and wires them together.
+This rule keeps the graph acyclic and lets Cargo build component crates in
+parallel.
+
+## Typed component event bus
+
+Use concrete types for application events. Producers publish these types into
+one event channel. The bus erases the type only inside its transport and
+subscription registries.
+
+```rust
+trait ApplicationEvent: Any + Send + Sync + 'static {}
+
+type SharedEvent = Arc<dyn Any + Send + Sync>;
+
+struct EventEnvelope {
+    event_type: TypeId,
+    value: SharedEvent,
+}
+```
+
+`Arc` permits one published value to reach more than one component without a
+payload copy. Each subscription keeps the event `TypeId`, an internal
+`ComponentId`, and an erased typed method function. The public API stays
+typed.
+
+The event bus keeps this index:
+
+```text
+TypeId of RepositoryChanged -> FilesComponent
+TypeId of GuideChanged      -> GuideComponent, FilesComponent
+TypeId of RevisionChanged   -> FilesComponent, DiffComponent, GuideComponent
+```
+
+The UI thread receives `EventEnvelope` values from the Crossbeam channel and
+asks the event bus to dispatch them. A timer publishes ticks only when a timed
+feature is active.
+
+The terminal reader is the only code that calls Crossterm event reads. It
+normalizes key and mouse values before it sends them. This prevents components
+from competing for terminal input.
+
+Repository, LSP, Herdr, and guide integrations keep their worker ownership.
+Adapters publish concrete result types. Do not make a component wait on a
+worker receiver.
+
+## Component registration
+
+The shared component trait provides the typed registration method. A
+component keeps its subscription declarations beside its handler methods.
+
+```rust
+trait Component<A>: Sized + 'static
+where
+    A: Send + 'static,
+{
+    fn register_subscriptions(
+        subscriptions: &mut ComponentSubscriptions<'_, Self, A>,
+    );
+}
+```
+
+`IntoDispatchResult<A>` keeps handler return values typed before dispatch
+erases them. Support these results:
+
+- `()` for an external event that only changes component state;
+- `Vec<A>` for an external event that also requests runtime work.
+
+The erased method function always returns one internal `DispatchResult<A>`.
+The event bus gives this result back to `ReviewApplication`. The application
+collects its actions.
+
+For example:
+
+```rust
+impl Component<Action> for FilesComponent {
+    fn register_subscriptions(
+        subscriptions: &mut ComponentSubscriptions<'_, Self, Action>,
+    ) {
+        subscriptions.subscribe(Self::repository_changed);
+        subscriptions.subscribe(Self::review_state_saved);
+        subscriptions.subscribe(Self::revision_changed);
+    }
+}
+
+impl FilesComponent {
+    fn revision_changed(&mut self, event: &RevisionChanged) {
+        // Update component state.
+    }
+}
+```
+
+Rust method values use `Self::method`. The registration must not use a closure
+that captures `self`. The bus stores a method function and looks up the
+component only when it dispatches an event.
+
+`subscribe` adds an application-event subscription to the bus-owned type
+index. `subscribe_input` adds routed input with an explicit scope and matcher:
+
+```rust
+subscriptions.subscribe(Self::repository_changed);
+subscriptions.subscribe_input(
+    InputScope::Focused,
+    FilesShortcutMatcher::new(),
+    Self::shortcut,
+);
+subscriptions.subscribe_input(InputScope::Hovered, AnyInput, Self::mouse_pressed);
+```
+
+The matcher selects the input before the handler runs. It reports no match,
+an incomplete input sequence, or a match with the typed value for the handler.
+The handler only updates state and returns actions. Each subscription owns its
+matcher state, including a pending multi-key shortcut.
+
+Components do not own identifiers or subscription-lifecycle state. The bus
+owns both. Removing one exact `ComponentTarget` removes only that instance and
+its subscriptions. Component lookup also requires the target so multiple
+instances of one concrete component type are not ambiguous.
+
+A component that emits events stores a narrow `EventPublisher`. This handle
+only adds an event to the same bus queue. External producers publish through
+`ComponentEventBus`. Both paths use one event queue and one typed subscription
+index. `ReviewApplication` owns the bus.
+
+Mount a component in this order:
+
+1. Reserve its internal component ID.
+2. Create its narrow `EventPublisher`.
+3. Construct the component.
+4. Register its static subscription declarations in the bus-owned index.
+5. Insert it into `ComponentEventBus`.
+
+The bus must not deliver events during this sequence. A single
+`mount` method enforces the sequence. Dispatch checks both the component type
+and event type before it calls the stored method.
+
+The subscription index is `HashMap<TypeId, Vec<Subscription>>`. The bus does
+not scan subscriptions for unrelated event types. One publication can produce
+more component events. The bus stops and reports an error when one publication
+causes more than 1,024 event deliveries. This prevents an accidental event
+cycle from using one CPU core without a limit.
+
+This model gives each registered handler a compiler-checked event type. It
+does not try to prove that a component registered every event that a feature
+needs. Subscription declarations stay explicit and local to the component.
+
+## Input delivery
+
+Route input in this order:
+
+1. Send keyboard input to the active modal, or to the focused component when
+   there is no modal.
+2. Send keyboard input that has no focused match to global shortcut
+   components.
+3. Send pointer input to the hovered component.
+
+Keyboard input goes to the modal component, or to the focused component when
+there is no modal. Global input subscribers receive keyboard input when no
+focused subscription matches it. The hovered input subscriber receives
+pointer input.
+
+```rust
+subscriptions.subscribe_input(
+    InputScope::Focused,
+    FilesShortcutMatcher::new(),
+    Self::shortcut,
+);
+subscriptions.subscribe_input(InputScope::Hovered, AnyInput, Self::mouse_pressed);
+subscriptions.subscribe_input(
+    InputScope::Global,
+    HelpShortcutMatcher::new(),
+    Self::open_help,
+);
+```
+
+`ReviewApplication` owns overlay state, focus, and component areas. It selects
+the focused or hovered target. The event bus tests that target's subscription
+matchers and invokes only matching handlers. It then tests global input
+subscribers when no focused matcher matches. An incomplete global multi-key
+sequence keeps the next key in global matching. The event bus does not read
+application layout or focus state.
+
+External event subscriptions do not use focus. The event bus invokes every
+component that declares the concrete event type and returns all requested
+actions to `ReviewApplication`. Registration order defines dispatch order.
+Components must not depend on this order for independent state updates.
+
+## Actions and external work
+
+Keep actions as requests from the UI to the runtime. A component can return an
+action, but it must not execute the action itself.
+
+Examples include:
+
+- load a diff;
+- load source text;
+- query or restart the LSP;
+- change a review state;
+- generate a review guide;
+- write to the selected output target;
+- edit a revision;
+- save a setting; and
+- quit.
+
+Split the current global `Action` enum only when component ownership makes a
+smaller action type useful. Each small action enum can convert into a runtime
+action. Do not copy payload types between enums.
+
+## Application coordinator
+
+Replace the current all-owning `ReviewApp` with a small coordinator. It owns:
+
+- the component instances;
+- current focus;
+- terminal dimensions and component areas;
+- application shortcut fallback routing;
+- cross-component navigation requests;
+- the repository revision identity that all visible components share.
+
+It must not own file-tree rows, a diff cursor, search state, guide items,
+popup scroll, or revision selector state.
+
+The coordinator and components publish concrete events for cross-component
+communication. For example, a file selection publishes `FileSelected`, and a
+guide update publishes `GuidePathIndexChanged`. The event bus delivers these
+events to the components that subscribed to their types.
+
+## Files component
+
+`FilesComponent` owns:
+
+- file summaries;
+- the file tree;
+- collapsed directories;
+- selected file;
+- pane scroll;
+- optimistic and pending review-state changes; and
+- file-pane rendering.
+
+It handles:
+
+- file navigation keys;
+- directory expansion;
+- file-pane mouse events;
+- repository snapshots;
+- completed review-state writes; and
+- comment-marker summaries from `GuideComponent`.
+
+It emits actions to load the selected diff and to change a review state. A
+selection change also publishes `FileSelected` for `DiffComponent`.
+
+## Diff component
+
+`DiffComponent` owns:
+
+- loaded diff documents by path;
+- the displayed document and optional preview;
+- cursor, column, and scroll state for each document;
+- selection and search state;
+- source locations and temporary source previews;
+- location history and LSP location lists; and
+- diff-pane rendering.
+
+It handles:
+
+- diff and source navigation keys;
+- search and selection input;
+- diff-pane mouse events;
+- loaded diff and source results;
+- LSP results.
+
+It emits diff, source, LSP, and output actions.
+
+`DiffComponent` contains small state types for selection, search, source
+preview, and LSP navigation. These types keep independent behavior out of the
+main component methods. The overlay host owns hover and context-menu state.
+
+Split the present `ReviewFile` before this move:
+
+- `FileSummary` contains path, display path, disk path, change kind,
+  statistics, review status, and temporary-file metadata.
+- `DiffDocument` contains the presented content, loading state, cursor, column,
+  scroll, and source location.
+
+Reuse repository and guide types for payloads. Do not create local copies.
+
+## Guide component
+
+`GuideComponent` owns:
+
+- review guide items and their path index;
+- previous and next item navigation;
+- item counters;
+- guide generation state; and
+- guide box rendering inside the diff area.
+
+It handles guide mailbox updates, guide navigation input, and its animation
+ticks. It emits guide-generation actions and small comment-marker summaries
+for `FilesComponent`.
+
+Guide navigation handlers subscribe to global input. The focused component
+gets the first opportunity to match each shortcut. During rendering,
+`DiffComponent` draws the document first. `GuideComponent` then draws guide
+boxes over the supplied diff area. It does not borrow all `DiffComponent`
+state. The coordinator gives it one `DisplayedDiffViewport` value. This value
+contains the displayed path, presented line positions, and scroll information
+that guide rendering needs.
+
+## Revision component
+
+`RevisionComponent` owns:
+
+- revision loading state;
+- candidate revisions;
+- selected candidate and popup scroll;
+- the pending edit target; and
+- revision selector rendering.
+
+It handles parent and child navigation input, candidate results, and revision
+edit results. It emits candidate-load and revision-edit actions. The
+application coordinator handles the final change of shared revision identity
+and tells the files and diff components to reset or restore their state.
+
+## Status component
+
+`StatusComponent` owns:
+
+- output target selection;
+- temporary status text;
+- guide generation animation display; and
+- footer rendering.
+
+It handles its keyboard and mouse shortcuts, saved setting updates, and timed
+status changes. Other components send small status events. They do not expose
+their complete state to the footer.
+
+## Overlay component
+
+Use one overlay host for modal input order and stacking. Keep each overlay as
+a concrete child with its own state and rendering:
+
+- shortcut help;
+- commit message;
+- hover documentation;
+- context menu; and
+- toast display.
+
+Do not force these children through a trait unless the host needs a common
+collection. An enum is sufficient for a fixed set of overlays.
+
+## Rendering
+
+Each component renders from its own state into a supplied `Rect` and
+`Buffer` or `Frame`. A view must not accept `&ReviewApplication`.
+
+Shared immutable rendering data, such as the palette, can be passed through a
+small rendering context. Do not copy theme state into every component only to
+avoid one immutable borrow.
+
+The application computes layout once per draw and gives each component its
+area. It then renders overlays in a fixed order.
+
+Preserve the current redraw schedule during this refactor. Redraw suppression
+is a separate performance change and is not required for component ownership.
+
+## Migration sequence
+
+### 1. Component library and central channel
+
+- Create `crates/component-core` for reusable component infrastructure.
+- Add the Crossbeam dependency to `reviewer`.
+- Introduce `ApplicationEvent`, `EventEnvelope`, and one central receiver.
+- Introduce `ComponentEventBus`, opaque `ComponentTarget` values, a narrow
+  `EventPublisher`, and the typed subscription API.
+- Store event types and handler methods in an erased internal subscription,
+  but keep the registration and publishing APIs typed.
+- Implement mount, dispatch, unsubscription, and component removal as one
+  tested lifecycle.
+- Move Crossterm reads to one producer.
+- Adapt worker, repository-watch, LSP, Herdr, guide, stop, resize, and timer
+  results into concrete event types on the same receiver.
+- Preserve the current `ReviewApp::update` call during this step.
+- Preserve the current timer and redraw behavior until component dispatch is
+  complete.
+- Keep concrete review events and actions out of `component-core`.
+- Make its component, event-bus, publisher, handler-result, and dispatch types
+  generic over the application action type.
+
+### 2. Shared UI protocols and application shell
+
+Slice 1 test inventory:
+
+- Adapted the existing runtime event-loop test to use the central event
+  channel.
+- Kept the existing action-dispatch, mouse-normalization, Herdr event-stream,
+  guide-flow, and repository-watch tests at their current runtime boundaries.
+- Added component lifecycle tests for typed dispatch, action results,
+  target-based removal, and type mismatch rejection.
+- Added a runtime test for terminal-reader shutdown while it waits for input.
+- Added runtime tests for central-channel routing, joined LSP adapter shutdown,
+  and cancellation of an idle Herdr event subscription.
+
+- Create `crates/ui-events` for concrete cross-component events and
+  `crates/ui-actions` for external-work requests when the first extracted
+  component requires these shared protocols.
+- Introduce scoped input subscriptions with subscription-owned matchers.
+- Move component focus, component areas, subscription eligibility, and ordered
+  input delivery into `ReviewApplication`. Keep the legacy files/diff focus and
+  pane layout with the temporary child until those two panes become separate
+  components in slices 3 and 4.
+- Keep existing state in a temporary legacy child while components move.
+- Mount the legacy child through the same `Component` trait and event bus.
+
+Slice 2 test inventory:
+
+- Added ordered input-delivery coverage that checks that matched focused input
+  stops delivery before global handlers.
+- Added application-shell coverage that sends keyboard input through the
+  mounted legacy component and checks the returned runtime action.
+- Added ordered keyboard and pointer-area routing coverage through the
+  application input boundary.
+- Added runtime coverage that checks that an ordered action batch does not
+  lose work before a quit action.
+- Added application-shell rendering coverage through a Ratatui test backend.
+- Kept the existing review UI state and rendering tests at their current
+  public boundaries while the legacy component remains mounted.
+
+### 3. File and document submodels
+
+- Introduce explicit `FileSummary` and `DiffDocument` submodels inside the
+  transitional `ReviewFile` aggregate.
+- Do not use dereference forwarding between the submodels. Call sites must
+  state which model they use.
+- Make refresh logic replace summaries without clearing the matching document.
+- Keep current behavior tests at the public application boundary. The files
+  component slice moves summary behavior tests to component input and output
+  boundaries.
+
+Slice 3 test inventory:
+
+- Kept repository refresh coverage that checks that a selected diff stays
+  visible until its replacement arrives.
+- Kept refresh coverage that checks that a change to another file preserves
+  the selected diff and its guide.
+- Kept refresh coverage that checks that cursor and viewport positions survive
+  a summary replacement.
+- Kept file-list, diff-rendering, navigation, and input tests at the existing
+  application boundary. Slice 4 must port summary-specific cases to component
+  inputs and rendered outputs.
+
+### 4. Files component
+
+- Create `crates/components/files` with the package name `files-component`.
+- Move file summaries, tree state, selection, review updates, input handlers,
+  and rendering together.
+- Remove the transitional `ReviewFile` aggregate. Store loaded documents by
+  path in the legacy diff state until `DiffComponent` owns them.
+- Register repository and file-pane handlers in `FilesComponent`.
+- Remove corresponding fields and methods from the legacy app state.
+- Keep its dependencies limited to the component library, shared protocols,
+  and the domain crates that its behavior uses.
+
+Slice 4 test inventory:
+
+- Ported file navigation, path insertion, optimistic review updates, failed
+  review restoration, directory collapse, parent expansion, guide markers,
+  file statistics, and layout rendering to component or composed-application
+  inputs and visible outputs.
+- Kept cross-component selection and diff-loading checks at the application
+  boundary.
+- Added temporary-source synchronization coverage so an external source is
+  visible in the files component and leaves it when a repository file is
+  selected.
+- Removed legacy file-state assertions after equivalent component coverage
+  was in place.
+
+### 5. Diff component
+
+- Create `crates/components/diff` and `crates/components/guide` with the
+  package names `diff-component` and `guide-component`.
+- Move diff document state, source navigation, search, selection, LSP state,
+  input handling, and rendering together.
+- Move guide state, guide events, and guide rendering into `GuideComponent`.
+- Reject stale external results in their owning typed component handlers by
+  revision identity and path.
+- Remove `input.rs` and `navigation.rs` behavior that moved into the
+  component.
+- Keep both crates independent. Exchange displayed-viewport and guide-index
+  values through shared event types instead of a component dependency.
+
+### 6. Revision, status, and overlay components
+
+- Create the `revision-component`, `status-component`, and
+  `overlay-component` packages in their matching `crates/components`
+  directories.
+- Move each remaining state and event group to its concrete component.
+- Replace whole-application borrows in views with component state borrows.
+- Keep only shared coordination in `ReviewApplication`.
+
+### 7. Remove the legacy shell
+
+- Remove the old `Message` routing and all temporary adapters.
+- Remove unused state and broad visibility.
+- Review module and crate names against their final contents.
+- Measure production and test size by component crate.
+- Split any component crate again when it contains independent parts with a
+  one-way dependency boundary.
+- Confirm that no component crate depends on `review-ui` or another component
+  crate.
+
+## Testing
+
+Keep each component's tests in its adjacent `.tests.rs` file. Keep every test
+module declaration at the bottom of its source file.
+
+Test components through their normal boundary. Generate keyboard, mouse, and
+external input events and deliver them through the component dispatcher.
+Check observable outputs: rendered test buffers, emitted application events,
+returned runtime actions, and routed input results. Do not call handler
+methods directly, inspect private component fields, or assert internal helper
+state. A refactor that preserves the same inputs and outputs must not require
+component test changes.
+
+Port existing tests with the behavior that moves into each component. Before
+each migration slice, list the current tests that cover the behavior in that
+slice. Move or adapt those tests to the new component API before removing the
+old implementation. Preserve their scenarios and important assertions. Do
+not replace an existing behavior test only with a smaller test of a new helper.
+
+Put tests for behavior owned by one component in that component crate. Keep
+cross-component tests in `review-ui` and external-work tests in `reviewer`.
+When an existing test cannot move unchanged, record why and keep equivalent
+coverage at the nearest stable boundary. Each migration slice must show which
+existing tests it ported and which new tests it added.
+
+Add focused tests for:
+
+- input matching and fallback order;
+- keyboard focus and mouse-area routing;
+- typed external-event subscriptions and dispatch;
+- removal of a component and all subscriptions for its exact target;
+- rejection of mismatched component and event types inside erased dispatch;
+- action emission;
+- state preservation across repository refreshes;
+- file selection and diff loading;
+- cursor and scroll preservation per file;
+- guide and LSP results for current and stale revisions;
+- modal input precedence;
+- guide input precedence and rendering over a displayed diff; and
+- clean shutdown while workers are active.
+
+Use Ratatui test buffers for component rendering. Keep runtime tests for event
+channel integration and action execution. Do not mock the component event
+loop when an existing real worker or test repository can provide the event.
+
+## Completion criteria
+
+- No render type borrows the complete application state.
+- No central input file contains behavior for every component.
+- Each visible component owns its state, event reactions, and rendering.
+- Reusable component infrastructure is in `component-core`, separate from
+  product component crates.
+- Independent product components are separate crates with no dependencies on
+  each other.
+- `review-ui` only composes components and owns application-wide coordination.
+- Each component declares typed subscriptions beside its handler methods.
+- No central match statement lists external-event destinations.
+- Removing a component removes all of its subscriptions.
+- External work still runs outside the UI thread.
+- One event receiver wakes the UI for terminal and external changes.
+- Current shortcuts and visible behavior remain unchanged.
+- Existing behavior tests move with their owning components, with equivalent
+  coverage for tests that cannot move unchanged.
+- Component tests generate events through the normal dispatch boundary and
+  check observable outputs instead of internal state.
+- Repository refreshes preserve the displayed file, cursor, and scroll when
+  their anchors still exist.
+- `make check` passes after every migration change.
+- Each migration change passes `$code-review` against its own fixed point and
+  follows the repository small-feature workflow.
+
+## Migration record
+
+### Diff model prerequisite
+
+Ported the three syntax-highlighting tests and the five diff-presentation
+tests from `review-ui` to `diff-component`. The presentation tests now build
+their input through `SyntaxHighlighter` and check the public presentation
+output. This removes their former direct construction of private highlighted
+rows and presentation state. This prerequisite adds no new test scenario.
+
+### Diff state and guide viewport projection
+
+Mounted `DiffComponent` beside the files and guide components. It now owns
+repository diff documents and publishes the displayed viewport data that the
+guide component uses. Exact `ReviewCheckpoint` values reject stale diff load
+results.
+
+Added component-boundary tests for loaded diff publication, stale-result
+rejection, cursor movement, and reviewed-file filtering. Kept the existing
+application tests for guide jumps, repository refresh preservation, and
+rendering because those behaviors cross the diff and guide component
+boundaries.
+
+### Diff-owned file decorations
+
+Moved notice and search-match file decoration calculation to `DiffComponent`,
+which calculates these values from its loaded documents. Ported the
+search-path assertions to a component-boundary test that sends keyboard input
+and checks the published file decorations.
+
+### Complete component rendering boundary
+
+Mounted the locations, status, overlay, and revision components. The
+application frame now composes only mounted component renderers. Removed the old header, footer, files,
+review, popup, help, hover, context-menu, and commit-message view modules.
+Also removed the legacy `ReviewApp::view` entry point and the copied diff
+viewport renderer. Input hit testing now uses the viewport projection from
+`diff-component`, so rendering and pointer coordinates cannot use different
+row models.
+
+Ported the application rendering tests to render `ReviewApplication` through
+the component frame. Kept cross-component visual scenarios at the application
+boundary. Component-specific visual tests remain in each component crate and
+send input or external events through the dispatcher before they check the
+rendered output.
+
+The final slice removed the temporary controller and its state bridge. The
+owning components now handle file selection, diff cursor and scroll,
+selections, source views, output targets, overlays, and their input directly.
+The application keeps only component composition and cross-component
+coordination.
+
+The files, status, and locations components use the shared event projections
+directly. They do not keep local copies of the file overview or location-list
+selection models.
+
+Guide composition uses two render passes. The diff component first draws the
+document and returns the positioned guide layer for the composed viewport.
+The guide component then draws that layer. This keeps guide text and borders
+owned by `GuideComponent` while the diff component keeps source-row wrapping,
+scrolling, and hit testing.
